## Windows side

### 1. BitLocker recovery key, lost PIN, password, and browser sessions
- **What happened:** After Omarchy finished installing, I explored it a bit,
  then booted into Windows to check it still worked and hadn't been wiped. I
  was met with a BitLocker recovery screen. Past that, my PIN had been reset,
  so Windows asked for my laptop's user password instead. I couldn't remember
  it, and none of my usual passwords worked, so I'm not sure if I had the
  wrong password or something else was going on. Once I was back in, my
  browser cache had also been cleared, and I had to log back in to every
  website.
- **Cause:** Changing Secure Boot in the BIOS made Windows treat the system as
  changed. This triggered BitLocker recovery, reset my sign-in PIN, and
  appears to have cleared my saved browser sessions as well. In hindsight, I
  likely disabled more in the BIOS than was strictly necessary. Omarchy only
  requires Secure Boot **or** TPM to be off, not both, and I had changed more
  than that.
- **Fix:**
  - **BitLocker:** I retrieved my recovery key from `aka.ms/myrecoverykey` by
    signing in with my Microsoft account on another device. Entering the key
    got me past the recovery screen with no data loss.
  - **Password:** I used Windows' built-in accessibility-based local account
    recovery method to reset my password from the lock screen.
  - **Browser sessions:** Logged back in to each site manually.
- **Lesson:** A single BIOS change triggered four problems at once. Before
  touching Secure Boot or TPM settings:
  1. Back up your BitLocker recovery key.
  2. Make sure you know your full account password, not just your PIN.
  3. Have a second recovery method ready (a Microsoft account email, or
     security questions).
  4. Only disable what the installer actually requires, not everything that
     might be related.

### 2. 1 TB drive unusable in Windows
- **What happened:** After the installation, I checked Disk Management and
  couldn't find a usable 1 TB drive. I had originally planned to use it as
  shared storage between Omarchy and Windows.
- **Cause:** Omarchy formatted the drive as LUKS-encrypted Btrfs, which
  Windows can't read natively. Disk Management shows it only as an "Unknown
  Partition".
- **Fix:** I wasn't able to make it readable from Windows. The drive stayed
  Omarchy-only for the rest of this attempt.
- **Lesson:** If you want a drive usable from both systems, create a separate
  small partition formatted as exFAT or NTFS for shared files, rather than
  giving the installer the whole drive as a Linux-only, encrypted target.

## Omarchy side

### 3. Caps Lock not working
- **What happened:** Caps Lock worked fine at the login screen but did
  nothing inside Hyprland. Pressing it just typed a `.` that disappeared.
- **Cause:** My Hyprland keyboard config (`~/.config/hypr/input.lua`) had
  `compose:caps` set in `kb_options`, which turns Caps Lock into a Compose
  key instead of a normal toggle. The whole config block had also been left
  commented out at one point, so an earlier attempt to fix it hadn't actually
  applied.
- **Fix:** I edited `~/.config/hypr/input.lua`, uncommented the config block,
  removed `compose:caps`, and kept:
  ```lua
  kb_options = "shift:both_capslock_cancel,grp:alts_toggle",
  ```
  Then reloaded with:
  ```bash
  hyprctl reload
  ```
- **Bonus fix:** The same block had `kb_variant = "intl"` set, which turned my
  apostrophe key into a dead key. Removing that line fixed normal typing too.
- **Lesson:** Omarchy's keyboard config is Lua, not a plain config file, and
  an entire block can be silently commented out. Check the file's actual
  contents with `cat` before assuming a setting isn't applied.

### 4. Videos glitching in Chromium-based browsers
- **What happened:** YouTube and other video in Chrome and Chromium-based
  browsers glitched: the player would go black, ads and the skip button were
  missing, tabs would disappear, and Spotify's interface was oddly zoomed in.
  Firefox played video fine, so it was specific to Chromium-based browsers.
- **Cause:** My laptop has hybrid graphics (AMD iGPU + NVIDIA RTX 4050).
  Checking `chrome://gpu` showed the browser was rendering on the AMD GPU, not
  NVIDIA, and hardware video decode on that path was the source of the
  glitching. All the right NVIDIA packages (`nvidia-open-dkms`, `nvidia-utils`,
  `egl-wayland`, `libva-nvidia-driver`) were already installed, so it wasn't a
  missing-driver issue. Forcing Chromium onto the NVIDIA render node directly
  just crashed it.
- **Fix:** Disabled hardware video decode for each Chromium-based browser by
  adding a flag to its flags file, then fully quitting and relaunching it:
  ```bash
  # Chromium
  echo "--disable-accelerated-video-decode" >> ~/.config/chromium-flags.conf
  # Brave
  echo "--disable-accelerated-video-decode" >> ~/.config/brave-flags.conf
  ```
  This didn't work for Google Chrome specifically, so I removed Chrome and
  switched to Brave instead.
- **Lesson:** On hybrid-graphics laptops, check `chrome://gpu` to see which
  GPU a Chromium-based browser is actually using before assuming a driver is
  missing. Firefox not having the same bug is a good way to confirm it's
  browser-specific rather than a system-wide GPU problem.

### 5. Hyprland glitchy
- **What happened:** General instability and glitchiness in the Hyprland
  session, beyond just the browser video issue above.
- **Cause:** Likely the same root cause as the video glitching and the NVIDIA
  driver trouble below: Hyprland is a Wayland compositor, and NVIDIA's
  proprietary driver on hybrid graphics is a rough combination that needs
  careful setup (correct driver package, `egl-wayland`, and environment
  variables pointing at the right GPU).
- **Fix:** Partly improved after installing the correct NVIDIA packages and
  setting environment variables in the Hyprland config, but it was never
  fully smooth for the rest of this attempt.
- **Lesson:** NVIDIA plus Wayland plus hybrid graphics is still one of the
  rougher combinations in the Linux desktop space. It's worth researching
  before picking a Wayland-based distro on an NVIDIA laptop.

### 6. NVIDIA driver trouble
- **What happened:** My laptop's NVIDIA RTX 4050 (alongside an AMD iGPU)
  caused repeated friction under Hyprland, contributing to the video glitching
  and general instability above.
- **Cause:** Hyprland needs the open NVIDIA driver (`nvidia-open-dkms`,
  `nvidia-utils`), `egl-wayland`, and correct environment variables to work
  properly on hybrid graphics. Even with those installed, some rough edges
  remained.
- **Fix:** Installed the recommended driver stack, which helped, but the
  combination was never fully stable. This was one of the reasons I was open
  to trying a different distro.
- **Lesson:** On a hybrid-graphics laptop, running the desktop on the AMD iGPU
  and reserving NVIDIA for heavy tasks is the more stable approach than
  letting everything default to NVIDIA under Wayland.

## Firmware and hardware

### 7. BIOS password set by accident
- **What happened:** Pressing F2 to enter BIOS setup later showed an "Enter
  current password" prompt I didn't remember setting.
- **Cause:** On Acer BIOS, the Secure Boot option is often greyed out until a
  Supervisor Password is set. I likely created one without realising it while
  changing Secure Boot earlier, and then forgot it.
- **Fix:** It turned out not to block anything important: the password only
  locks BIOS setup (F2), not booting. I confirmed the system was still in the
  state I expected using `tpm.msc` (TPM ready for use) and `msinfo32` (Secure
  Boot State: Off), so no BIOS access was actually needed. I properly reset
  the BIOS password during my second attempt (Fedora).
- **Lesson:** Write down any BIOS/firmware password immediately when you set
  one, even by accident. If you get locked out, check what it actually
  restricts before panicking: it may not block booting at all.

### 8. Laptop wouldn't turn on, plus battery replacement
- **What happened:** At one point the laptop wouldn't turn on: the fans spun
  and the keyboard lit up, but nothing showed on screen. Separately, the
  battery stopped holding a charge and needed replacing.
- **Cause:** The "won't turn on" scare traced back to mashing F2 repeatedly
  during the boot logo transition and forcing a power-off, which can corrupt
  firmware boot variables. The battery issue appears to be a separate,
  unrelated hardware fault rather than anything caused by the dual boot.
- **Fix:** A repair shop resolved the boot issue simply by plugging the
  laptop in and powering it on; no part replacement was needed. For the
  battery, I ordered a replacement (part **AP21D8M**, not the AP18E7M/AP18E8M
  listings sold for older Nitro 5/Predator models, which aren't compatible)
  and confirmed it matched my Nitro ANV15-41 before buying.
- **Lesson:** Press BIOS keys (F2/F12) once and wait, rather than mashing them
  during a logo transition, especially if you force a power-off afterwards.
  When buying a replacement battery, double-check the part number against
  your exact model rather than trusting a listing's title.
